ZeroClaw architecture plan

Project shape
- Use Vite + TypeScript as a client-only app with strict compiler settings.
- Keep domain logic framework-independent so board behavior can be unit-tested without the DOM.
- Use Vitest for model/state tests and Playwright for end-to-end desktop and mobile flows.

Module structure
- src/main.ts: Vite entrypoint, mounts the top-level app.
- src/App.ts: top-level controller wiring game state, persistence, settings, and UI events.
- src/types.ts: shared exported types for Cell, Board, Difficulty, GameStatus, MoveResult, Hint, PointerIntent, Settings, and BestTime.
- src/game/board.ts: board creation, coordinate helpers, bounds checks, adjacency computation.
- src/game/mines.ts: first-click-safe mine placement.
- src/game/reveal.ts: reveal behavior and flood-fill for empty regions.
- src/game/marks.ts: flag/question-mark cycling.
- src/game/state.ts: game state machine, transitions, timer hooks, win/loss detection.
- src/game/hints.ts: deterministic hint selection from current visible board state.
- src/input/longPress.ts: long-press detector with cancellable timers.
- src/input/pointer.ts: unified mouse/touch/pen intent mapping.
- src/storage/bestTimes.ts: localStorage-backed best-times tracker with validation.
- src/audio/sounds.ts: small sound-effects facade with mute support and graceful no-audio fallback.
- src/ui/Layout.ts, BoardView.ts, StatusBar.ts, DifficultySelector.ts, InstructionsPanel.ts, Overlay.ts, HintButton.ts, ThemeToggle.ts, SoundToggle.ts: focused UI modules.
- src/styles/*.css: base tokens, layout, board, controls, themes.
- tests/unit/*.test.ts: model, state, input, storage, and edge-case tests.
- tests/e2e/*.spec.ts: Playwright desktop and mobile coverage.

Shared types
- Coordinates should use zero-based row/col pairs and helper functions rather than string parsing at call sites.
- Cell state should separate immutable mine/adjacency data from mutable reveal/mark state.
- Public game actions should return typed results so UI can render status, effects, hints, and overlays without duplicating game rules.
- Difficulty presets and custom-board validation should share one typed config path.

Issue dependencies
- #1 is the base for all implementation work.
- #2, #3, and #4 establish quality gates; #3 depends on #1, #4 depends on #1 and the eventual UI.
- #5 must precede game logic and UI contracts.
- #6 precedes #7 through #11 because all board behavior depends on coordinates and adjacency.
- #7, #8, #9, and #10 form the core game loop; #10 depends on reveal, marks, and mine placement.
- #11 depends on visible board state from #6 through #10.
- #12 and #13 can be developed after #5, then integrated into #15 and #19.
- #14 establishes visual foundations before #15 through #18 and #21 through #23.
- #15 through #18 build the primary UI on top of the game model.
- #19 wires the app once core logic, input, and main UI pieces exist.
- #20, #21, #22, and #23 are independent enhancements after #19 defines integration points.
- #24 and #25 should be written after the app is playable through #19, then expanded for enhancements.
- #26 and #27 should happen after the full feature set is present.
- #28 is last and verifies production build, test commands, and deployment documentation.

Key design decisions
- Prefer pure functions for board and game logic; keep DOM, timers, audio, and localStorage behind thin adapters.
- Generate mines only after the first reveal so the initial click and its adjacent cells can be kept safe when board size permits.
- Use an explicit game status enum: ready, playing, won, lost. Timer behavior derives from status transitions.
- Model marks as none -> flag -> question -> none to support the requested cycling behavior.
- Use CSS variables for theme tokens so dark mode can switch without duplicating component styles.
- Keep accessibility in mind from the first UI issue: buttons for interactive controls, ARIA labels for cells, keyboard navigation hooks, and status text that can be announced.
- Validate custom board sizes and mine counts in one shared helper used by UI, tests, and future persistence.
- Keep deployment docs lightweight: include install, test, build, preview, and static hosting notes once the production build is verified.
